name: blender-modeling
description: Create, import, or edit Rumi meshes using background Blender Python scripts, then inspect and iterate in the Three.js viewer. Use for model creation and geometry edits.
---

# Blender modeling

Call \`blender_status\` for the executable and helper paths. Run Blender through the ordinary shell tool, using its permissions and cancellation; do not build a separate execution service. If Blender is unavailable, report the setup action returned by the tool.

## Start from the joints kit

For any model with more than one part, or with a wheel, hinge, slider, snap or gear, read \`joints\` first and build with its kit. It supplies millimetre primitives, cleaned Boolean operations, joint recipes and \`K.publish\`, which writes the whole USD assembly, so the script needs no \`configure_robot\`. Design moving parts for [separate printing and assembly](../joints/SKILL.md#separate-printing-for-moving-parts); this is required, not a choice. Revisions, the viewer and validation below apply unchanged.

## Solid construction defaults

Use these defaults from the first modeling operation, including edits to existing models. They reduce mesh defects but do not establish printability.

- Build each physical part from closed solid primitives or capped extrusions of simple, non-self-intersecting profiles with positive thickness. Prefer these methods over manually assembling vertices and faces; avoid zero-thickness surfaces, duplicate geometry and internal faces.
- Use Boolean union to fuse material and difference to cut holes, with \`solver = 'EXACT'\`. Give solids intended for union meaningful overlap and extend through-hole cutters beyond both outer surfaces. Avoid tangent-only connections and unnecessarily coincident boundaries; inspect the result even with the Exact solver.
- Joining Blender objects does not fuse their geometry. Union pieces intended to form one solid, while keeping separate mechanical parts and their clearances distinct.
- Establish the main solid before bevels and other finishing operations. Keep bevel widths compatible with nearby features and wall thickness; inspect the evaluated result for collapsed features and self-intersections.
- Reserve voxel remeshing for organic geometry where changes to topology, sharpness and detail are acceptable. Preserve the original, choose a voxel size that resolves the smallest required feature, and recheck dimensions. Do not use it as routine repair for threads, mating surfaces, thin walls or moving-part clearances, or remesh separate moving parts into one solid.
- Inspect evaluated geometry after significant topology changes, before building further features on it. \`Mesh.validate()\` checks and can repair mesh data; it does not establish manifoldness. Use 3D Print Toolbox findings for geometry diagnosis. Prefer correcting the construction that caused a defect over broad automatic cleanup; inspect any cleanup result for lost features and changed dimensions. Before completion, follow the validation workflow below.

## Edit and publish

1. Read the current \`meshState\` context and the existing project files. Create another model folder only when the user explicitly requests or agrees to a separate model; clarify ambiguous intent before writing files. For edits and fixes, publish revisions in the existing model folder. Preserve existing models. For an existing Blender model, find its latest successful revision report under \`models/<name>/revisions/\` and open that report's \`blend\` path. Use the selected revision when the user asks to revert. For a USD, legacy GLB or STL import, use the helper's \`import_mesh\`. Legacy JavaScript previews are disabled by project isolation; migrate their actual geometry to USD using confined tools rather than approximating it from screenshots. Do not overwrite their source.
2. Write a reproducible Python script under \`source/\`. Run \`"<executable>" --background --factory-startup --python-exit-code 1 --python "<script>"\`. This runs a full Blender process without a window; ordinary system Python cannot import \`bpy\`.
3. Work in **metres, Z up** in Blender. Rumi's viewer, measurements and Gaussian regions use **millimetres, Z up**. Divide model-local cursor/highlight coordinates and radii by 1,000 before using them in Blender; account for object transforms when selecting vertices. Standard USD export/import handles the USD coordinate frame. Do not apply viewer placement to an editing selection twice.
4. Follow the solid construction defaults above when choosing Blender modifiers and mesh operations. For symmetric objects construct one half and mirror it about an explicit plane; measure symmetry after evaluated modifiers. Keep planar faces planar, bevel widths consistent, sharp boundaries deliberate, and smooth normals confined to intended smooth surfaces. More subdivisions or smooth shading alone do not correct a lumpy silhouette. Inspect front, side and three-quarter views. Distinguish topology checks from visual/reference fidelity.
5. Load the helper returned by \`blender_status\` with \`helper = runpy.run_path(helper_path)\`, then call \`helper["publish"]("models/<name>")\`. Use \`runpy\` so importing the helper does not write bytecode into the installed application. It saves an immutable \`.blend\` and USD revision, prints the paths and \`RUMI_CHECKS\`, and atomically updates \`model.usda\`. Keep named parts, modifiers, and movement declarations in the editable original. Preview limits are 200,000 triangles and 64 MiB; preserve detailed assets separately if a lighter preview is required. Do not silently simplify the print deliverable.
6. Call \`viewer_load\` on \`models/<name>/model.usda\`. Saved models display independently of validation. Use \`RUMI_CHECKS\` and \`meshState.validation\` to inspect findings; a visible model is not a printability pass. Use \`viewer_camera\`, \`viewer_cursor_snapshot\`, and \`viewer_highlight\` for iteration.

## Efficient geometry edits

For rim or outline proximity, use the joints kit's \`K.outline_distances(points, outlines)\` instead of Python loops over every vertex and segment. Pass finite 2D coordinates in one consistent unit; outlines are closed automatically. The returned NumPy array gives exact point-to-segment distances in the same unit, including zero-length segments. For an X/Z rim band in millimetres: \`distances = K.outline_distances([(v.co.x * 1000, v.co.z * 1000) for v in bm.verts], outlines_mm)\`, then select \`[v for v, distance in zip(bm.verts, distances) if distance < band_mm]\`. Account for object transforms when the outline is not in mesh-local coordinates. Keep the same smoothing operations, topology and publish checks.

Reuse the editable original of the intended revision when changing only one feature; do not regenerate unrelated geometry. For visual inspection use \`viewer_camera\` and \`viewer_cursor_snapshot\` before starting separate Blender renders. Preserve full geometry and clearance checks on each published edit.

To revert to an existing saved revision, call \`helper["restore_revision"]("models/<name>", "<revision-directory-name>")\` instead of rebuilding it. Identify the intended revision from its report and preview; do not guess from directory ordering. This atomically restores the exact saved USD, retains all revisions and returns the matching \`.blend\` path. It leaves the current Blender scene unchanged, so open the returned \`.blend\` before further edits. Call \`viewer_load\` on the canonical \`model.usda\` afterward. Restoration leaves validation pending; use the explicit validation tools before claiming acceptance or readiness.

## Declare physical movement

Every multi-part or movable model requires a complete assembly description. Give each physical piece a unique mesh name and include retaining pins, caps and axles. Disconnected solids cannot become one rigid piece by grouping or joining their mesh objects. Preserve requested features, connectors, names, controls and movement ranges through repairs and conversions.

Use standard OpenUSD Python APIs in a \`configure_robot(stage)\` callback. The exporter creates \`IsaacRobotAPI\` on \`/Model\`, applies \`IsaacLinkAPI\`, \`PhysicsRigidBodyAPI\` and \`PhysicsCollisionAPI\` to each mesh, and lists the links in \`isaac:physics:robotLinks\`. Set that ordered relationship explicitly so the first link is the intended test fixture. Author every connection with \`UsdPhysics\`, apply \`IsaacJointAPI\`, and list every joint in \`isaac:physics:robotJoints\`:

\`\`\`python
from pxr import Gf, Sdf, UsdPhysics

def configure_robot(stage):
    root = stage.GetDefaultPrim()
    root.GetAttribute("rumi:manufacturingMode").Set("separate")
    root.GetRelationship("isaac:physics:robotLinks").SetTargets(["/Model/Frame", "/Model/Door"])
    joint = UsdPhysics.RevoluteJoint.Define(stage, "/Model/DoorHinge")
    joint.GetPrim().AddAppliedSchema("IsaacJointAPI")
    joint.CreateBody0Rel().SetTargets(["/Model/Frame"])
    joint.CreateBody1Rel().SetTargets(["/Model/Door"])
    joint.CreateAxisAttr("Z")
    joint.CreateLocalPos0Attr(Gf.Vec3f(0, 0, .012))
    joint.CreateLocalPos1Attr(Gf.Vec3f(0, 0, .012))
    joint.CreateLowerLimitAttr(0)
    joint.CreateUpperLimitAttr(90)
    joint.GetPrim().CreateAttribute("rumi:control", Sdf.ValueTypeNames.String, custom=False).Set("Door")
    root.GetRelationship("isaac:physics:robotJoints").SetTargets([joint.GetPath()])

helper["publish"]("models/<name>", configure_robot=configure_robot)
\`\`\`

This example assumes both mesh transforms are identity and requires actual connectors that retain the door and can be assembled after separate printing. Joint positions and rotations are local to the respective body: calculate each frame from the saved world pivot and axis. USD positions and prismatic limits use metres; revolute limits use degrees. The current checker supports fixed, revolute and prismatic joints, at most 128 parts and 128 joints, with finite ranges containing the saved pose at zero and bounded by ±180 degrees or ±1 metre. Include every retaining pin, cap and axle as its own link. The first robot link is held by the fixture; all others must be connected through the declared graph. Joints describe expected behavior; Bullet does not create ideal constraints that could hide missing connectors.

The helper retains the native USD description in the editable \`.blend\`. Later \`publish\` calls preserve it; supply \`configure_robot\` when changing the robot. USD is the authoritative geometry and joint format, while \`.blend\` retains editable modifiers. Use one self-contained ASCII layer, metres/Z up, with no external textures, references, variants or animation. Use surface colours for the preview. Each exported physical link must be one uniquely named mesh.

Rumi-specific attributes cover information the robot schema does not define: \`rumi:control\` on a joint labels its slider; \`rumi:manufacturingMode\` on the root must be \`separate\` for moving assemblies. Legacy \`print-in-place\` models remain viewable but require connector redesign before publishing or print preparation. Optional root strings \`rumi:sequences\` and \`rumi:disassembly\` encode JSON movement waypoints and translation-only reverse-assembly paths. Waypoints name joint prims and use degrees or millimetres; disassembly paths name link prims and use millimetre offsets. These are checking instructions, not a second body/joint description. Unsupported coupling and incomplete coverage remain unverified. See [movement controls](#movement-controls) for viewer widgets.

## Movement controls

Use [interactive-controls](../interactive-controls/SKILL.md) to choose widgets and labels. Publish control layout changes in a model revision. Operating those controls afterward changes only the local preview pose, not saved geometry or validation input; pose changes need no freeze or save. Keep physical joint declarations and connectors intact when editing the UI.

Without UI declarations, revolute and prismatic joints generate sliders labeled by \`rumi:control\` or the child mesh name. To choose widgets, set the default prim’s string attribute \`rumi:motionControls\` to a JSON array. Each item has \`kind\`, \`label\`, and \`axes\`; each axis references an absolute joint prim path and may set \`step\`. For example:

\`\`\`python
controls = [dict(kind="pad", label="Aim", axes=[
    dict(joint="/Model/Joints/Pan", step=1),
    dict(joint="/Model/Joints/Tilt", step=1),
])]
K.publish("models/turret", root=base, joints=joints, mode="separate",
          steps=steps, controls=controls)
\`\`\`

The kit writes joints under \`/Model/Joints/<USD identifier of joint id>\`. Use the actual exported paths. For direct USD authoring, set \`root.CreateAttribute("rumi:motionControls", Sdf.ValueTypeNames.String, custom=False).Set(json.dumps(controls))\` inside \`configure_robot\`.

A slider or curve references one movable joint; a pad references two independent movements, horizontal then vertical. Joint limits supply axis ranges; reset returns to the saved pose at zero. Steps use degrees for rotation and millimetres for translation; defaults are 1 degree or 0.1 mm, capped to the range width. UI declarations cannot override limits or reset. Unspecified movements retain automatic sliders.

Joints sharing \`rumi:control\` share one value and must use matching units and overlapping limits. Reference that group once through any member joint. Repeated bindings, missing or fixed joints, unsupported widgets, and invalid steps withhold movement controls with a viewer diagnostic; geometry stays visible. The preview supports trees with up to 12 independent movements and preserves fixed descendants. Closed loops remain unsupported.

After publishing, inspect \`viewer_load\` feedback under \`design.motion\` and exercise each axis and reset. Invalid UI metadata does not change the physical validation result. Preview movement does not establish mechanical feasibility. Temporary mesh edits disable movement controls while keeping the panel visible. Reset the pose before source-bound cursor edits. Compatible pose values survive revisions and model switching while the viewer remains mounted; reopening starts at the saved pose.

## Clearances and retention

Keep at least 0.3 mm between separate parts everywhere in the saved pose: radial gaps in bores, end-play and door-to-frame gaps. \`RUMI_CHECKS\` reports \`insufficient_clearance\` as an error below 0.2 mm and lists the pairs and gaps. Parts joined by a fixed joint may touch.

Play is expected. Retention passes when the connector stops the part; the pivot may travel up to 2 mm and tilt up to 15°. Never reduce clearances to reduce drift.

Repair \`unretained_part\` and \`unstable_at_rest\` by adding or enlarging the feature that captures the part: a cap, shoulder, flange or collar wider than the bore, or a pin through both knuckles. Do not tighten the fit. Repair \`insufficient_clearance\` by widening the listed gaps while keeping the capturing feature.

## Pointed regions and printing

Use \`printer-awareness\` before supplying a build volume or making printer-specific fit, nozzle or filament claims.

Use the cursor snapshot associated with the request and visually ground named regions through \`viewer_highlight\`, following \`clarify-mesh-changes\`. Check the model revision before editing; a Gaussian region is a soft reference, not an exact vertex selection or semantic part boundary. Changes to topology invalidate old triangle indices.

Keep \`.blend\` files as editable originals and USD as the shared geometry and robot description. Export print meshes separately from the intended evaluated solids and verify units, wall thickness, manifoldness, fit, supports and slicer output using \`fdm-design\`. Blender operations and viewer checks do not certify print readiness. Whole-model placement still uses \`viewer_transform\`; it does not rewrite the Blender original.

## Validate a finished revision

Every \`publish\` checks the new revision in a separate Blender process and prints \`RUMI_CHECKS <json>\`: Toolbox geometry findings, part clearance, the assembly declaration error if any, and \`dimensionsMm\`. Only findings with a nonzero count appear. Read it after each build and fix \`error\` severity findings before building further; \`warning\` findings need judgment against the intended design. \`status: "unavailable"\` means no checks ran; call \`blender_status\` once in this project and publish again. Write no separate manifold, clearance or collision scripts, and do not invoke installed validator scripts or Bullet by hand.

The open viewer automatically checks the selected saved revision and cancels superseded checks; it does not accept revisions or start repair turns. Explicit tools reuse completed stages. When the design is ready and \`RUMI_CHECKS\` has no blocking errors, call \`validation_mechanics\` once with the model \`path\`. The host re-runs any missing cheap checks for that revision first and stops with their findings if one blocks. Otherwise it runs Bullet, the costly stage (up to ten minutes), testing whole-assembly contacts, retention, sampled movement, declared sequences and removal paths with only the root held. Do not weaken settings, omit parts, narrow movement or substitute a reduced assembly to obtain a pass; incomplete coverage remains unverified. Then call \`validation_accept\` with the \`path\` and \`fingerprint\` it returned. For moving assemblies, declare removal paths for every nonroot part; missing paths produce \`assembly_path_required\` and block acceptance while retaining the motion findings. Only an \`accepted\` result supports an acceptance claim, and it does not certify slicing, deformation, strength, assembly accessibility or print readiness. A printer volume that was never configured means printer fit is unchecked.

After any repair or placement change, publish again and repeat for the new revision; never report a previous revision's results as current. Validation never starts follow-up turns, so finish repairs in this turn. Preserve requested features, connectors, controls and full movement ranges. If checks fail or cannot be resolved safely, report the remaining limitation briefly instead of claiming acceptance.
",
  "clarification": "---
name: clarify-mesh-changes
description: Establish which region of a 3D model a person means and what change they want. Use visual references when words such as this part, around the eyes, bigger, or rounder leave materially different edits possible.
---

# Clarify mesh changes

Use ordinary language and the visible model to establish a shared reference. Use a highlight to visually ground region-specific edits and named parts. Follow clear requests, including undo and selections from choices you offered. Ask for clarification when the intended region or action is ambiguous; do not require a special confirmation phrase.

## Identify the region

For a continuous planar cut, use the guide provided by \`viewer_controls\` with operation \`cut\` as the visual reference. It traces the actual live cut across the selected parts and moves with the controls. Do not arrange separate Gaussian blobs around the rim. Opening cut controls clears old blobs; resolve the cut before highlighting a different region. A \`rendered\` control result confirms the cut guide.

If the spoken request includes a \`cursor-at-start-of-spoken-request\` reference, use that image and region for its pointing words. It was requested at the first transcript chunk, not at exact audio onset; compare its request and capture times. Do not replace it with wherever the cursor moved later. If it failed or is ambiguous, ask for a new reference. Otherwise, when the person refers to the region they are pointing at, call \`viewer_cursor_snapshot\` for the pinned blue region or a fresh hover hit. Cursor mesh vision is blue; agent-proposed regions are teal. A captured result contains an image, capture time, pointed part, world-space Gaussian sphere, and model identity. An editable-model hit also includes \`region.modelLocalBlob\`: pass it as one blob to \`viewer_highlight\`, copying \`region.selection\` too when present, with \`identity.design.path\`, \`revision\`, and \`url\`, to show your own proposed region. Do not move the camera before taking the snapshot. The capture occurs when the viewer processes the request, through an immediate viewer wakeup; it does not recover where the cursor was when the person spoke. On \`no_hit\`, ask them to click the intended surface to pin it. On \`viewer_unavailable\`, use the reported reason to tell them what needs attention. Ask them to open or bring forward the preview when it is closed or hidden. Ask them to reset Movement when the preview is posed, resolve active editing controls, or exit the print layout as applicable. On \`stale_view\` or \`capture_failed\`, resolve the viewer issue and capture again; never substitute an earlier region. A snapshot does not approve an edit.

Read the current \`meshState\` context and inspect the selected model's source before choosing coordinates. Whenever you refer to a part such as a chin, jaw, cheek, eye, rim, or crease, first show that part with \`viewer_highlight\`, except when the continuous cut-line guide already shows the requested cut. Explain it as the teal highlighted area with a simple positional description. Do not assume the person knows anatomical or modelling terms. For several distinct parts, highlight and discuss one at a time; a combined highlight is appropriate only when the person is choosing the combined edit region. Named parts can help identify a region, but one mesh may contain many meaningful features. Describe left and right from the person's current view or use an unambiguous feature; do not silently switch between the wearer's and viewer's perspective.

For an editable model loaded with \`viewer_load\`, call \`viewer_highlight\` with the exact \`design.path\`, \`design.revision\`, and \`design.url\` from the current \`meshState\` context. Give the region a short descriptive label. Supply a few ellipsoidal blobs with \`center\`, \`radii\`, and \`rotationDegrees\`, each a three-number array. Coordinates are millimetres in the model's local frame, after part transforms but before whole-design placement. Rotation uses XYZ Euler degrees. Radii specify nominal extent, not diameter or Gaussian standard deviation. The viewer blends their influence with a fixed soft fade and reveals wireframe within the region.

Start with the smallest collection that covers the intended area. A flattened, tilted blob can cover an eyebrow; two blobs can indicate both eyes without covering the entire forehead. Inspect depth as well as width and height: a blob can also intersect the back of a thin shell. This is an approximate conversational region, not a precise edit mask or a selection of entire connected parts.

The tool waits for a matching receipt from the visible viewer. Only \`rendered\` confirms rendering; \`not_rendered\` or \`superseded\` does not. On failure, explain that the highlight is not confirmed, ask the person to open the preview, and retry. Never say “I have highlighted it” before a \`rendered\` result. Rendering still does not establish that the person saw or approved it. Use their reply in context, including corrections or a choice you offered. On \`stale_model\`, read the state again and recompute against the new source; resolve build errors first. Highlights are temporary and clear when the source revision changes or another model loads. Imported STL objects do not support this tool.

## Establish the change

Ask one short question in the conversation about the uncertainty that matters, then wait for a spoken or typed reply. Do not use a questionnaire or require clicking an option. For an eye region, distinguish enlarging the openings from widening the surrounding material. Establish whether bigger means wider, taller, thicker, or more raised when the context does not settle it. Do not infer exact edit boundaries from the soft highlight.

When the person corrects the region, stop work based on the previous region, replace the complete blob list, and clarify any remaining ambiguity. Keep their requested change in context. Do not make unrelated improvements while waiting. Hiding the highlight is a viewing preference, not approval. Confirmation of a region is not authorization for an unrelated change or a print.

When the requested change is clear, follow \`mesh-edit-controls\` if the person wants to adjust it themselves; prepare a faithful live binding without applying their edit first. For a direct edit, change the model source and inspect the resulting preview and measurements. Clear an obsolete reference with \`blobs: []\`, using the current model identity and an empty label. Explain changes using the person's feature names rather than coordinates or triangle indices. Only refer to labels or viewer controls that actually exist; do not invent hover labels.

Cursor driven mesh highlighting uses local patch, connected surface, whole part, and boundary edge scopes. A captured \`region.selection\` identifies the exact part path and geometric surface for this source revision; copy it with \`modelLocalBlob\` for teal confirmation. Surface boundaries follow creases, material seams, and disconnected pieces, not inferred anatomical names. Short voice phrases such as “whole panel”, “whole part”, “just the edge”, “larger selection”, and “smaller selection” refine the original pointing reference locally. Use the refined attached snapshot; never substitute a later hover. For bilateral changes, inspect the model’s actual symmetry and highlight both intended regions; do not assume its symmetry axis or silently treat one side as both. Selection phrases change presentation only and never authorize an unrelated geometry edit.

## Worked examples

**Clear request, no ambiguity.** "Make the eyes bigger." Region is settled by the model's face; see it, call \`viewer_highlight\` on both eye regions in one highlight because both are meant, and move on to the edit. Do not ask a question you do not need answered.

**Ambiguous size word.** "Make the eyes bigger." The openings might grow, or the surrounding material might widen. Highlight the openings, describe them as the teal area, and ask one question: "Should the eye openings get larger, or the material around them get wider?" Wait for the reply before computing anything.

**Ambiguous region.** "Make this part rounder", with the cursor over the shoulder. The pointing reference narrows it, but "rounder" has several meanings. Capture with \`viewer_cursor_snapshot\`, highlight the pointed region, then ask whether they mean the outer contour or the local bulge. One question, not a list.

**Correction.** The person said "the chin", you highlighted the whole jaw, and they say "no, just the chin". Stop work on the jaw region, replace the complete blob list with a smaller chin-only highlight, and confirm the new region in plain words. Keep the change they want in context; the correction concerns the region, not the request.

**Pointing with nothing captured.** The person points and says "this bit's too thin", but the capture returns \`no_hit\`. Ask them to keep the pointer over the intended surface and retry. Do not guess a region from the words alone, and do not reuse an earlier capture.

**Authoritative but unrelated.** The person says "yes, that's the right spot" and then "now delete the eyes completely". Confirming the region authorized the highlight, not a geometry deletion. Treat the deletion as a new request with its own ambiguity.

## Asking well

Ask about the uncertainty that changes the edit, not about things you can observe.

- Ask: "Wider or taller?" when both are plausible and produce different geometry.
- Ask: "Which side?" when the model is not symmetric and the request names one feature.
- Do not ask: "Is this the chin?" when a highlight already shows them the chin.
- Do not ask: "Should I proceed?" Confirmation of a region is not a confirmation step; follow the person's clear request when it is clear.
",
  "cursor": "Ask the open 3D viewer for a PNG and the pinned blue cursor region or a fresh hover hit. A pin can be captured while the window is hidden. Prefer an attached cursor-at-start-of-spoken-request reference for that utterance. The result includes model identity, camera, capture time, part, point and radius in millimetres, selection scope, and anchorId. For an editable model, copy region.selection and modelLocalBlob into viewer_highlight with identity.design path/revision/url to show the proposed area in teal. Read the image and region before highlighting or editing. On no_hit, ask the person to pin a surface. On viewer_unavailable, read reason. Bring the preview into view for viewer_hidden, reset Movement for movement_preview, resolve controls for temporary_edit, or leave the print layout for print_layout. Ask the person to check the preview on response_timeout. Treat the region as a soft reference.",
  "fdm": "---
name: fdm-design
description: Apply FDM design constraints when creating or modifying meshes intended for physical printing. Use during design, before slicer preparation; the modeling method remains the agent's choice.
---

# Design for FDM printing

Preserve the requested design while considering these constraints. They guide design; this skill does not run checks or certify print readiness.

Use \`printer-awareness\` to obtain the selected printer, nozzle and material facts before applying printer-specific constraints.

## Core constraints

1. **Represent the intended solid.** Use explicit units, finite coordinates, and nondegenerate triangles. Each intended solid should have a closed, consistently oriented surface without self-intersections or nonmanifold connections. Slicer repair is not evidence that the intended geometry was preserved.
2. **Fit the usable printing region.** Check the proposed placement against the active nozzle's reachable region and exclusions. Reserve room for required supports, adhesion aids, and clearance. On dual-nozzle printers, do not substitute the combined envelope for either nozzle's usable region.
3. **Keep intended connections physical.** Features that must form one piece need a real material connection or an explicit assembly joint. Mere contact at a point or edge is not a reliable bond. Separate parts and color regions are allowed; shell count alone is not a defect.
4. **Plan support and bed attachment.** Choose an orientation with a viable first layer or an adhesion/support strategy. Regions above it need sufficient support from previous layers, feasible bridging, or generated supports. Temporary supports need a removal or dissolution route. Face angle alone does not establish support; a flat base and support-free printing are not universal requirements.
5. **Preserve printable features.** Size walls, posts, holes, gaps, and text for the selected nozzle, layer height, material, and wall generator. Thin features may disappear or widen during slicing. No fixed wall thickness, bridge length, or overhang angle applies to every configuration.
6. **Allow intended fits and motion.** Mating and moving parts need clearance or interference appropriate to the intended fit, material, orientation, and calibrated process. Do not assume nominally identical dimensions will fit. Keep at least 0.3 mm between separate moving parts; tighter fits fuse or bind. Use a small fit test when the result matters and calibration is unavailable.
7. **Design for intended use.** For functional parts, choose material, section thickness, connections, and orientation for expected loads and temperature exposure. Layer-dependent strength, shrinkage, and warping matter; successful slicing does not establish strength or dimensional accuracy.

## Sequence the work

Constraints only help when they arrive before the geometry is fixed. Work in this order.

1. **Get printer facts.** Read [printer-awareness](../printer-awareness/SKILL.md) for the selected model, nozzle diameter, single or dual mode, build volume and excluded areas. Without a selected printer, continue a visual draft and leave fit, feature size and clearance unverified rather than guessing defaults.
2. **Inspect the geometry you have.** Read the current \`meshState\` and the model's source. Record overall dimensions, the thinnest walls and posts, hole diameters, gap sizes, and which parts must move or stay separate. These numbers decide every constraint below; do not assess them from the render alone.
3. **Choose an orientation.** Pick the resting face for the first layer, then check what the choice costs: supported area, bridge spans, layer direction against expected load, and whether surface finish matters on the face touching the bed. Orientation is a design decision made now, not a slicer setting fixed later.
4. **Apply the constraints that the facts and the geometry disagree on.** Each numbered constraint above names a decision. When a requested design cannot meet one, say which and why, and offer the smallest change that resolves it before building.
5. **Validate before claiming readiness.** Run the model checks described in [blender-modeling](../blender-modeling/SKILL.md#validate-a-finished-revision), then slice and inspect the preview for unsupported regions, thin walls and layer time. Report what was checked and what remains unverified.

## Report the checks before recommending a change

Reading these constraints is not applying them. When a requested design could fail to print — a feature near the nozzle limit, a span that needs support, a fit that must move, a part loaded in service, or a size near the bed limit — fill in the table below and let its answers shape the recommendation. Skip the table when nothing in the model is near a limit: a small solid shape in a known material needs no ceremony.

Write a number in every Cell column. If you cannot, write \`unverified\` and say what fact is missing. A row answered "looks fine" is a row not done.

| Ask | Cell | Answer |
|---|---|---|
| What can the nozzle actually place? | nozzle diameter × layers | e.g. \`0.4 mm → 0.8 mm min wall\` |
| Smallest wall, post or stroke in the model | measured value vs that cell | e.g. \`0.6 mm < 0.8 mm — too thin\` |
| Smallest hole or gap | measured value vs nozzle | e.g. \`3.0 mm — prints ~2.8 mm, drill after\` |
| Every moving pair | measured gap vs 0.3 mm | e.g. \`0.25 mm — binds, raise to 0.35\` |
| Overhangs and bridges | angle or span that needs support | e.g. \`55° overhang, 12 mm bridge\` |
| Orientation chosen | what it costs | e.g. \`flat — no support, 3 mm layer lines across load\` |
| Overall size | vs usable bed region | e.g. \`120×40×30 in a 220×220 bed\` |
| Facet held in service | material and load assumption | e.g. \`PLA, ~2 kg static — fine\` |

Then answer in this order: what must change, why each change follows from its row, and which rows stayed \`unverified\`. Do not lead with a change whose row you have not filled.

If the person supplied no printer, material or layer height, say so once and mark the affected rows \`unverified\` rather than assuming a common setup. Naming the gap is more useful than a confident answer built on guessed defaults.

## Worked examples

**A wheel that must spin.** The person asks for a cart with four turning wheels on a 0.4 mm nozzle. Constraint 6 sets 0.3 mm between the wheel bore and the axle, and constraint 3 means the axle is a separate part rather than a fused lump. Orientation puts the wheel disc flat on the bed so the bore is round, not ovalised by layer stair-stepping. The joint kit in [joints](../joints/SKILL.md) already carries these clearances; read it before hand-modelling the axle.

**A bracket that must hold.** The person asks for a shelf bracket "as thin as possible". Constraint 7 makes the load the deciding fact, not the render. Lay it so layer lines run along the bending span, since a part loaded across layer boundaries splits sooner. State the assumption you used for load and offer a thicker section if it is heavy.

**Filling the table for a bracket.** Dimensions 120 × 40 × 30 mm, 2 mm walls, one 3 mm pin hole, Ender 3, 0.4 mm nozzle, PLA, no layer height given. Rows: wall \`2 mm\` against \`0.8 mm min\` — fine; hole \`3 mm\` prints undersize to roughly \`2.8 mm\` — enlarge to \`3.3 mm\` or drill after; no moving pair — not applicable; upright is vertical so the hole's upper rim overhangs — make it a teardrop; orientation \`flat on the base flange\`, no support needed; size fits \`220 × 220\`. Layer height is \`unverified\`, so say that the wall figure assumes a coarse-enough layer to keep two perimeters, rather than asserting it.

**Text on a lid.** The person asks for their name embossed on a lid in the same colour. Constraint 5 makes the stroke width the question: letters narrower than about two extrusion widths on the selected nozzle may vanish or blur. Either raise the text to a relief height that survives, or widen the strokes, and say which you did.

**A part that will not fit the bed.** The person asks for a 300 mm rail on a 256 mm bed. Constraint 2 rules out printing it as one piece. Offer splitting into parts with a connection that constraint 3 can justify — a dovetail or a pinned joint, not a butt seam — or a different orientation if the diagonal fits. Do not scale it down without being asked.

## When a constraint cannot be met

These are the recurring failures with their causes and the action to take.

| Symptom | Cause | What to do |
|---|---|---|
| Slicer reports open or nonmanifold edges | Geometry was not a closed solid, or booleans left stray faces | Repair the source geometry and re-check; do not accept slicer auto-repair as proof |
| Thin wall or post vanishes in the preview | Feature thinner than the nozzle can deposit | Thicken to at least two extrusion widths, or remove the feature and say so |
| Small hole closes up | Hole smaller than the nozzle, or bridging across the top | Enlarge the hole, or model it as a teardrop and drill after printing |
| Moving part fuses or binds | Clearance below the calibrated process tolerance | Raise clearance to 0.3 mm or more, re-check, and consider a fit test coupon |
| Part warps or lifts at a corner | Large flat area, sharp corner, or uneven cooling | Add a brim, round the corner, or reconsider material; report that the cause is process, not geometry |
| Two parts meant to be one separate | Only touching at a point or edge | Add a real overlap, a connector, or an explicit assembly joint |
| Long unsupported span droops | Bridge span beyond the material's feasible length | Reorient, add supports, or split the span with a rib |
| Model will not validate after a change | A boolean left intersecting triangles or a degenerate face | Rerun the operation with cleaned inputs, or repair the affected region; a new defect is not excused by an older one |

Report a constraint you could not satisfy, and why, rather than silently certifying the result. A model that slices is not a model that fits, holds or prints.
",
  "highlight": "Replace temporary Gaussian wireframe regions on the editable model loaded by viewer_load. Read displayedDesign in the current meshState context (design when validation is unavailable) for the visible path, revision and url; inspect model source to locate regions. Coordinates are model-local millimetres before viewer_transform, including part transforms. Rotation uses XYZ Euler degrees. Up to eight ellipsoids combine by maximum influence, with a fixed soft fade. All intersected surfaces, including the back, may be highlighted. Empty blobs clears. Does not edit geometry or exports. Waits for the visible viewer to acknowledge the matching highlight. Returns rendered only after acknowledgment; not_rendered or superseded is not success. Rendering is not user confirmation. Use this tool to visually ground region-specific edits and explanations of named parts. Follow the user’s requested action and clarify ambiguous intent; no special confirmation phrase is required. Use clarify-mesh-changes for guidance.",
  "instructions": "### Project type

This project is for designing and creating 3d prints.

### Project plugin

The primary plugin to enable that is print-studio, a Cordis plugin.

The plugin provides the ability to design, iteratively, the 3d mesh through its accompanying renderer.

The plugin provides the ability to prep and print the mesh to the user's personal 3D printer.

### Editable designs

Use background Blender Python scripts for saved mesh assets and Three.js for viewing or parameterized interactive models. When someone asks to adjust a model themselves, read \`mesh-edit-controls\`, infer the desired geometric variation and preserved features, and choose or construct faithful bindings before exposing controls. Generate model-specific fields when appropriate; do not depend on prepared presets or silently substitute a completed edit. Call \`blender_status\`, then read the \`blender-modeling\` skill. Keep editable \`.blend\` originals and immutable revisions under \`models/<name>/revisions/\`; publish \`models/<name>/model.usda\` with the provided helper. Create a separate model only after an explicit user request or agreement; otherwise edit the existing model and keep its revisions in the same model folder. Preserve existing models when adding an authorized model. The viewer discovers model folders and reloads completed USD exports automatically.

Blender works in metres, Z up. Standard USD files use metres, Z up; the viewer converts them to millimetres, Z up. Cursor and highlight regions are model-local millimetres before viewer placement: divide by 1,000 for Blender and respect object transforms. Keep named parts and modifiers. Use the existing shell tool to execute scripts with Blender's \`--background --python-exit-code 1 --python\` options; no Blender window is needed.

Call \`viewer_load\` with a USD path to bring it into view; use the current \`meshState\` context for models, selection, load errors and measurements. Self-contained static USD previews support at most 64 MiB and 200,000 triangles. Keep detailed print originals when explicitly making a lighter preview. Failed exports or loads retain the last successful preview and are not completed edits. Use \`viewer_transform\` for placement and \`viewer_camera\` for the viewing angle. Preview checks do not establish print readiness.

Executable \`model.js\`/\`model.mjs\` previews are disabled by project isolation. Preserve them when migrating their actual geometry into Blender. Do not modify the viewer or approximate an existing model from a screenshot.

### Movable prints

Build physical connectors and publish a complete assembly for multi-part or movable models, following \`blender-modeling\`, \`joints\` and \`interactive-controls\`. All moving physical parts must print separately and assemble afterward; follow the separate-printing requirement in \`joints\` before designing connectors. Preserve rotation and sliding after assembly. Do not offer print-in-place, silently relabel captive geometry, or fix moving joints to bypass this requirement. Use a compatible \`joints\` recipe for each wheel, hinge, slider or snap before designing one by hand. Include every mesh, pin, cap, connection and movement control; preserve requested movement and full ranges during repairs. Keep at least 0.3 mm between separate parts and capture parts with connector geometry rather than tight fits; see \`blender-modeling\`. After each build, read the \`RUMI_CHECKS\` line that \`publish\` prints and fix blocking errors. When the design is ready, follow the validation workflow in \`blender-modeling\`: \`validation_mechanics\` once, then \`validation_accept\` with its fingerprint. The open viewer refreshes checks for the selected saved revision automatically; explicit validation tools reuse completed stages. No automatic repair turns run. Models remain visible while validation and repair run. Read the matching \`meshState.validation\` report before claiming acceptance. Unsupported or incomplete checks remain unverified; a probe pass does not certify material strength or print readiness.

### Model references

Use the request's captured model selection as a default for an unnamed model, with explicit names and captured pointing references taking precedence. Later browsing does not redirect ongoing work. Clarify conflicting references before editing. Call \`viewer_working_model\` with a discovered entry before inspecting or editing it; update the declaration when changing targets and clear it before clarification. The green dot identifies your declared work independently of the selected model and clears when your agent stops. Read the current \`meshState\` context for both selection and working models. Use \`viewer_load\` only to change what the person sees.

The viewer renders every source through USD in Three.js. Keep editable originals; Publish data-only USD from the confined modeling tools; STL imports are converted automatically. Declare assembly joints for movement controls; temporary edit controls use the supported USD deformation operations.
",
  "interactive": "---
name: interactive-controls
description: Choose and describe Rumi’s sliders, two-axis pads, and scalar curve widgets for mesh edits or joint movement.
---

# Interactive controls

Choose a widget after identifying what the person should be able to vary. Use [mesh-edit-controls](../mesh-edit-controls/SKILL.md) to design geometric edits and [blender-modeling](../blender-modeling/SKILL.md#movement-controls) to bind joint movement.

## Choose the widget

- \`slider\`: one independent numeric value.
- \`pad\`: two related spatial directions; the first axis is horizontal and the second vertical.
- \`curve\`: one numeric value adjusted by dragging the curve’s centered handle up or down. It does not provide draggable profile points or change the underlying mapping.

Prefer a slider unless the other gesture helps express the task. Name controls after their visible effect, using the person’s language. Expose implementation settings only when they help the person make the intended change. Labels do not create bindings. Do not promise numeric entry or units the widget and binding do not support.

## Declarations

A control has \`kind\`, \`label\`, and \`axes\`. Labels are nonempty and at most 80 characters. A slider or curve has one axis; a pad has two. A panel supports 1–12 controls. Bind each numeric value at most once.

The renderer’s axes have \`key\`, \`min\`, \`max\`, \`step\`, and \`reset\`. Values and ranges must be finite, \`min < max\`, and \`0 < step <= max - min\`. Initial and reset values must lie in range. Choose a useful range and a step small enough for deliberate adjustment. Reset restores the meaningful starting value.

Mesh-edit tools receive these axes directly. USD movement declarations instead reference joints; the viewer derives keys, limits, and reset values from those joints. Follow the relevant skill for the declaration format, delivery feedback, and save behavior.

Controls support keyboard input and per-control reset. Verify that each axis changes the intended feature and that reset restores its starting state. A rendered acknowledgment confirms delivery, not the quality of the interaction.

## Worked examples

**One value.** "Can I make the snout stick out more?" A single \`slider\` labelled "Snout length", one axis \`{key: 'amount', min: 0, max: 1, step: 0.01, reset: 0.5}\`. Amount .5 is the current shape for built-in deformations, so the person starts from what they already see.

**Two directions that belong together.** "I want to move the ears out and up." A \`pad\`, first axis horizontal for outward, second vertical for upward, both starting at .5 with equal ranges. Do not offer two sliders when the gesture is one spatial movement; the pad expresses the relationship.

**A shape over a range.** "Let me tune the curve of the back." A \`curve\` per adjustable station, each with its own label naming the station's position ("Back — upper", "Back — middle") rather than "Station 1". The curve handle adjusts one value; it does not expose movement points.

**A request the widget cannot keep.** "Let me type the exact millimetre value." A slider does not accept numeric entry. Say so and offer the closest thing: a fine step on the slider, or a direct source edit for an exact dimension. Do not label a slider with millimetres it cannot promise.

**Too many controls.** A request implies eight independent values across four panels. A panel supports 1–12 controls, but the person cannot reason about twelve at once. Choose the four that match how they described the change, and say which other values you fixed and at what value.

**Bind each value once.** If two controls would both drive the same underlying axis, bind it to one and give the other a different quantity, or drop it. Two controls on one value fight each other and make the preview unpredictable.
",
  "joints": "---
name: joints
description: Ready-made printable joints and a millimetre modelling kit for Blender scripts — wheels on axles, hinges, sliders, snap fits, gears. Read before modelling any part that moves or attaches to another part.
---

# Joints and the modelling kit

\`blender_status\` returns a \`joints\` path. Load it at the top of the build script:

\`\`\`python
import runpy, types
K = types.SimpleNamespace(**runpy.run_path(joints_path))
\`\`\`

The kit works in **millimetres, Z up** and creates the Blender geometry in metres for you. Do not mix raw \`bpy\` coordinates with kit calls. Parts keep identity transforms, which the joint frames need.

Use the kit and a recipe before designing a connector yourself. Working out retention geometry and joint frames by hand is the slowest part of a build; a recipe is one call.

## Kit

| Call | Makes |
|---|---|
| \`K.start()\` | An empty metric scene. Call once, first. |
| \`K.box(name, size, center)\`, \`K.box2(name, low, high)\` | A box by size and centre, or by two corners. |
| \`K.cylinder(name, radius, length, center, axis, radius2=None)\`, \`K.rod(name, radius, a, b)\` | A cylinder or cone frustum, by centre and axis or between two points. |
| \`K.sphere(name, radius, center)\`, \`K.prism(name, points, height, center, axis)\`, \`K.lathe(name, profile, center, axis)\` | A sphere, an extruded 2D outline (concave is fine), a revolved \`(radius, height)\` profile. |
| \`K.join(a, b, ...)\`, \`K.cut(a, tool, ...)\`, \`K.common(a, tool)\` | Fuse, subtract, intersect. The first part is changed and kept; the others are deleted. Results are cleaned for the geometry check. |
| \`K.copy(obj, name)\`, \`K.mirror(obj, 'X', name)\`, \`K.move(obj, offset)\`, \`K.rotate(obj, axis, degrees, pivot)\` | Duplicate, mirror across the origin plane, translate, turn. |
| \`K.paint(obj, (r, g, b))\` | One surface colour. |
| \`K.bounds(obj)\`, \`K.solids(obj)\` | Corner coordinates; the number of disconnected pieces (a printable part has one). |
| \`K.joint(name, kind, parent, child, axis, pivot, limits, control)\` | A joint record for a connector you modelled yourself. |
| \`K.publish(directory, root, joints, mode=..., controls=...)\` | Publishes \`model.usda\`, writes the whole USD assembly from the joint records, and prints \`RUMI_CHECKS\`. It replaces \`helper["publish"]\` and \`configure_robot\`. |

Axes are \`'X'\`, \`'-Y'\`, \`'+Z'\` or a vector. Give tools and helper solids names starting with \`_\`; never leave one in the scene, because every visible mesh is published as a part. Overlap pieces you fuse by at least 0.5 mm and run cutters past the surface.

## Separate printing for moving parts

Rumi requires every moving physical part to be printed separately and assembled afterward. Joint kind still describes movement after assembly: fixed, rotating or sliding. Do not offer print-in-place as an option or ask the user to choose a manufacturing mode. Publish movable assemblies with explicit \`mode='separate'\`; omitting the mode or using \`print-in-place\` is rejected. Single-piece static models need no manufacturing choice.

Design the assembly before choosing connectors. Provide accessible insertion paths, an assembly order and separate pins or retainers where needed. Explain it briefly: “The wheels and doors print separately, then attach so they still spin and open.” Consider bed orientation, support removal, joint clearances, materials, strength and repairability. Keep requested movement; never fuse a moving part or change it to a fixed joint to meet the requirement.

Recipes described below or in their cards as print-in-place are legacy geometry references, not supported designs for new movable prints. Do not use their captive defaults or change a manufacturing label to make them appear compatible. Use a separately assembleable variant only when the card identifies one, or design removable connectors with the kit and declare them with \`K.joint\`. Existing printed-assembled models remain inspectable, but their connectors must be reviewed and redesigned before republishing or arranging for printing. Preserve their previous revisions.

Run the geometry, clearance, collision, retention and movement workflow in [finished-revision validation](../blender-modeling/SKILL.md#validate-a-finished-revision). Describe the assembly order and pass \`steps=[dict(part="part_name", path=[[0, 0, 0], [dx, dy, dz], ...]), ...]\` to \`K.publish\` for every nonroot part. Offsets use millimetres and each path starts at zero. Missing paths leave the mechanical result under review and block acceptance, even when motion probes pass. Bullet checks supplied translation paths in reverse; it does not discover them or simulate snapping, flexing or threaded insertion. Missing or unsupported assembly evidence remains unverified even when movement checks pass. The \`separate\` declaration and plate placement are not proof that assembly is possible.

## Build order

1. Plan assembly after separate printing, then block out each physical part as a simple solid with its real outer size and position.
2. Choose each joint's pivot and axis from those coordinates.
3. Call the recipe. It adds and cuts the mating features on both parts and returns the joint record. A recipe raises an error that says what to move when a part does not reach the joint.
4. Add detail and colour. Build one side and \`K.mirror\` it for pairs.
5. \`K.publish(...)\`, read \`RUMI_CHECKS\`, then follow \`blender-modeling\` for \`viewer_load\` and validation.

Choose movement widgets through [blender-modeling](../blender-modeling/SKILL.md#movement-controls). Pass their declarations as \`controls\` to \`K.publish\`; joint geometry and limits remain the movement definition.

## Publish the assembly

After modeling separately printable parts and their connectors, publish their assembled pose with \`K.publish('models/kart', body, joints, mode='separate')\`. The viewer uses the joints for motion and creates a separate plate layout for printing. Keep every removable pin, cap and axle as its own physical part. Publishing does not create an insertion path or make a captive connector assembleable.

## Recipes are starting points

Each recipe was checked alone at its default size on a small sample; your whole model still has to pass its own checks. Resize a recipe through its options, and keep clearances and minimum walls as they are when you scale a model up or down. When no recipe fits, model the connector with the kit and declare it with \`K.joint\`; copy the nearest recipe's proportions.

What the mechanics check needs, whichever way you build:

- Only the root part is held. Every other part must be caught by shape in every direction: an assembled retainer wider than its bore, a removable pin secured by a cap, a lip over an insertable slider. Friction, press fits, magnets and glue hold nothing in the check.
- Provide retention geometry that can be assembled after separate printing. A join held only by friction stays unverified; report that limitation rather than switching to print-in-place to obtain a pass. Fuse parts with \`K.join\` only when the design calls for one physical piece.
- Keep 0.3 mm between different parts. Let flat faces and round pins in round bores carry loads; cone tips, slots and sharp edges in a bearing surface read as \`excessive_penetration\`.
- Recipes marked **over limit** below can read 0.16–0.39 mm \`excessive_penetration\` against the 0.15 mm limit while the part is captive. Do not tighten clearances or redesign repeatedly to chase it; after one repair attempt report the model as built and unverified.

## Catalog

Mechanics column: **passes** means the recipe's sample passed \`validation_mechanics\` at the default limits. **over limit** means the part is captive and passes rest and retention, but a probe read \`excessive_penetration\` just above 0.15 mm. **unverified** means the check cannot confirm it by design; say so to the user.

| Need | Recipe | Mechanics | Card |
|---|---|---|---|
| Wheel that spins on a body | \`K.wheel\` then \`K.captive_axle\` | passes | \`wheels.md\` |
| Two wheels turning together on one axle | \`K.through_axle\` | passes | \`wheels.md\` |
| Soft tyre on a rigid hub | \`K.tyre\` | passes | \`wheels.md\` |
| Wheel held on by a collar | \`K.axle_clip\` | passes (\`open_clip=True\` unverified) | \`wheels.md\` |
| Door, lid or flap | \`K.barrel_hinge\` | over limit, 0.16–0.39 mm | \`rotation.md\` |
| Door that clips onto a post | \`K.clip_hinge\` | over limit, 0.18–0.34 mm | \`rotation.md\` |
| Fold line in one flexible part | \`K.living_hinge\` | one part, nothing to check | \`rotation.md\` |
| Tilting head or arm | \`K.ball_socket\` | over limit, 0.27–0.35 mm; one tilt axis | \`rotation.md\` |
| Turntable, turret, spinner | \`K.turntable_plate\` then \`K.turntable\` | passes | \`rotation.md\` |
| Shaft turning inside a housing | \`K.bearing\` | passes | \`rotation.md\` |
| Sliding carriage or drawer | \`K.dovetail_slide\`, \`K.tslot_rail\` | passes | \`sliders.md\` |
| Telescoping tube, piston | \`K.telescoping_tube\` | passes | \`sliders.md\` |
| Latch or slider in a slot | \`K.pin_slot\` | passes | \`sliders.md\` |
| Snap two parts together | \`K.snap_hook\` | passes | \`fasteners.md\` |
| Round snap-on lid | \`K.snap_lid\` | passes | \`fasteners.md\` |
| Flat-pack wall into a base | \`K.tab_slot\` | over limit, 0.16 mm | \`fasteners.md\` |
| Twist-lock cap | \`K.bayonet_lock\` | over limit, 0.20 mm | \`fasteners.md\` |
| Printed bolt and nut | \`K.bolt_nut\` | unverified: the nut can unscrew | \`fasteners.md\` |
| Push-fit pegs, dog-bone key, magnets, metal insert | \`K.press_pegs\`, \`K.dogbone_key\`, \`K.magnet_pockets\`, \`K.insert_boss\` | unverified: held by friction, magnetism or hardware | \`fasteners.md\` |
| Meshing gears | \`K.gear_pair\` (blank: \`K.spur_gear\`) | passes up to ratio 1.45 | \`mechanisms.md\` |
| Rack and pinion | \`K.rack_pinion\` | passes | \`mechanisms.md\` |
| One-way ratchet | \`K.ratchet\` | passes; axis must be horizontal | \`mechanisms.md\` |
| Slider that clicks between positions | \`K.detent_slider\` | passes | \`mechanisms.md\` |
| Cam lifting a follower | \`K.cam_follower\` | over limit, 0.22 mm | \`mechanisms.md\` |
| Crank driving a slider | \`K.crank_slider\` | unverified: closed loop | \`mechanisms.md\` |
| One-piece spring | \`K.flexure_spring\` | one part, nothing to check | \`mechanisms.md\` |

Read the card before the first use of a recipe. Each family's file under \`references/\` beside this skill gives every recipe's call, what the parts must provide, the options worth changing, how it prints, and what validation reported. A recipe sample passing motion probes does not establish separate assembly feasibility.
",
  "load": "Select a Blender-exported USD in the Three.js viewer. Use models/<name>/model.usda; the viewer discovers these entries and reloads completed exports automatically. USD uses metres and Z up; the viewer converts to millimetres and Z up for measurements, placement and Gaussian regions. Use blender_status and the blender-modeling skill for editing with background Blender scripts. Keep .blend originals and revision exports. Self-contained static USD only, at most 64 MiB and 200,000 preview triangles. Preserve detailed originals when making a lighter preview. Executable .js/.mjs previews are disabled by project isolation; export USD instead. Reloading identical source bytes preserves staged controls and their review while requesting fresh renderer feedback. Changed source bytes invalidate them. Saved models display immediately; use validation_mechanics then validation_accept before claiming acceptance. Waits for renderer feedback for the exact source revision, placement and build volume. Returns load.status ready with design.report measurements and effective design.parameters, or an explicit build_failed, source_failed, viewer_hidden, timeout, superseded, cancelled or unavailable outcome. Only ready acknowledges a visible rendered model; timeout is not evidence of an unsupported model. Loading does not certify print readiness.",
  "meshEdits": "---
name: mesh-edit-controls
description: Turn a request to adjust a mesh into useful live controls. Choose the editable features, construct their deformations, verify the preview, and save or cancel the edit.
---

# Mesh edit controls

Use this skill when someone wants to adjust a shape themselves. A direct instruction such as “make it 20 mm wide” can be a source edit; a request to explore needs controls before applying the change. For existing joint movement, follow [blender-modeling](../blender-modeling/SKILL.md#movement-controls).

## Understand the edit

Read the current \`meshState\`, identify the intended model, and call \`viewer_working_model\`. Inspect the displayed revision’s source, dimensions, part positions, and construction. A named feature may be a region within one mesh or span several parts. Use [clarify-mesh-changes](../clarify-mesh-changes/SKILL.md) to ground the request visually. Ask one concrete question only when materially different interpretations remain.

Identify what should vary and what must stay fixed: openings, mating surfaces, wall surfaces, symmetry, connectors, and adjacent parts. Distinguish a profile change from local inflation and geometry edits from moving an existing part.

Choose a small set of useful adjustments. For each, define its geometric effect, affected region, neutral value, range, and protected features. Derive these choices from the request and current geometry; do not require prepared files, subject presets, or specific control names from the person. Source parameters help explain construction but are not automatically live bindings.

## Implement the adjustments

- Use a built-in operation only when its actual deformation matches the intended edit. Read [analog-shaping](../analog-shaping/SKILL.md) for bindings and regions.
- Use \`surface\` for a continuous side-view profile, radial band, groove, or planar patch when its geometry matches the request. Place profile stations from measured sections, choose the front direction, and pin stations around protected interfaces at zero travel rather than exposing those stations as controls. Link adjacent stations with the same displacement direction when a feature must move without changing its internal shape. Check crown continuity and the lower rim; a radial ring edit is not a whole-profile edit. Facets use target planes, not a smoother bulge. For an oriented hollow shell, supply the measured wall thickness so inner and outer faces target separate planes; avoid rim edges and verify the transition. Do not add a seated part to the edit merely to claim its fit is preserved.
- For other custom variations that preserve mesh connectivity, author [displacement fields](references/fields.md). Derive the mapping from the current construction, measured profile, or cross-sections. Evaluate source parameter changes on temporary copies when useful. Keep the generator in the project so it can be revised.
- New holes, count changes, and other unsupported live reconstruction need a specific explanation and an agreed alternative. Do not silently replace requested controls with static variants or a completed edit.

Check existing field files against the current revision and request before reusing them. Preserve the editable Blender original. Executable JavaScript previews and \`operation: parameters\` are unavailable.

## Verify and present

State the expected geometric effect and preserved features for each axis. Prepare the controls through \`viewer_controls\`; preparation stages them without changing the person's preview. Validate the prepared controls to generate actual-mesh endpoint and combination comparisons. Inspect every before/after image, displacement overlay, and measurement against the expected effects. Check useful movement, direction, continuity, pinching, intersections, part fit, and both sides of shells. Measure planarity for flat-face requests and wall thickness for shell edits; displacement magnitude alone does not establish either. An inverted or collapsed triangle fails surface validation: reduce the range, protect the affected region, or repair the source, then validate again. Compare defects against the saved mesh. New intersecting triangle pairs fail even beside an existing defect; a lower total count does not excuse new pairs. Check wall thickness against the project’s minimum, not an invented threshold. If preserving a defective region requires it, move that region rigidly or keep it fixed. Repairing the saved source is a separate edit. A completed render is evidence, not a quality pass.

Record an assessment for each axis and the combinations. Revise ineffective, redundant, or disruptive bindings, then prepare and validate again. Present controls only after accepting the current evidence. The tool tracks pending work and invalidates evidence when the model or bindings change; do not substitute formula samples or self-authored tests for its comparisons.

Read [interactive-controls](../interactive-controls/SKILL.md) to choose widgets and labels. Field values start at zero. For built-in deformations, amount .5 leaves the geometry unchanged; choose the other starting values as described in \`analog-shaping\`. Briefly explain what changes and what stays fixed.

Only \`rendered\` confirms visibility. For \`viewer_hidden\`, bring the window forward; the viewer retries. For \`render_failed\`, fix the reported binding or model error. A timeout does not establish that the requested edit is unsupported.

## Revise unsuitable controls

When the person says controls are wrong or ineffective, reread this skill and inspect the binding. Distinguish delivery failure, a bad region or range, and an unsuitable deformation. Revise the adjustments or representation; relabeling the same operation does not fix it.

Preserve wanted unsaved adjustments; ask if keeping them is unclear. Close an explicitly rejected preview without baking it. Keep the saved revision intact while preparing replacement controls. Dissatisfaction does not authorize a static edit or saving.

## Save or cancel

Keep controls open during exploration. Satisfaction or moving to another task resolves the latest visible edit first. Do not use the original opening values in place of the person’s adjustments.

1. Freeze with \`viewer_controls\`, \`action: freeze\`, and the current control \`id\`. A failed freeze does not authorize saving stale values.
2. Call \`blender_status\`, open the original \`.blend\` matching the selected USD, and load the returned helper with \`runpy\`. Call \`helper["apply_shape_controls"](recipePath)\`, then \`helper["publish"]\` and \`viewer_load\` the new revision. The helper checks the source USD hash and applies the frozen deformation; do not approximate it with another operator. It bakes evaluated modifiers on affected parts, so retain the original revision and modifier stack. Verify the published preview and that \`meshState\` has cleared the temporary edit and highlight before reporting a save.
3. For explicit cancel or revert, close the current \`id\` to restore the saved preview. If the source changes, reread state and reopen controls.

Failed previews preserve the last successful geometry and values. Failed execution leaves the original untouched on disk; report the error and allow reopening with the saved values. Unresolved controls are not a saved edit. A saved shape is not certified print-ready.

## Worked examples

**Exploration versus instruction.** "Make the nose 2 mm longer" is a direct source edit; do not build controls for it. "Let me try a few nose lengths" is a control request: one \`slider\` axis bound to nose length, range around the current value, neutral at the start. The wording decides which path.

**Choosing the axes.** "I want to change how tall and how wide the ears are." Two independent numerics: a \`slider\` for height and one for width, or a \`pad\` if they should move together as a two-axis feel. Protect the root where the ear meets the head by pinning it at zero travel rather than exposing a fourth control.

**A profile edit.** "Make the lid dome deeper but keep the rim flat." Use \`surface\`, place stations from the measured cross-section, pin the rim stations at zero travel, and link the dome stations to move together. Validate both endpoints and the combination, then check the rim has not bowed.

**An unsupported request.** "Add three more holes in a row." Live reconstruction of new topology is not supported. Explain what is unavailable and offer an alternative: a source edit producing a new revision, or a single hole with adjustable diameter if that covers the intent. Do not ship a static variant and call it a control.

**Rejected controls.** The person says the slider "does nothing". Distinguish the cases: no visible movement means a bad range or binding; visible movement in the wrong place means a bad region; visible wrong-shape movement means the deformation does not match the intent. Inspect before relabelling, because a new name on the same bad binding changes nothing.

## Deciding what is fixed

Before preparing anything, name the protected set. Typical protected features are mating surfaces, connector bores, wall thickness on shells, symmetry planes, and adjacent parts that must not move. A control that silently moves a protective feature is a defect even if it looks right in the preview.
",
  "printer": "---
name: printer-awareness
description: Retrieve printer identity, connectivity, live state, filament and machine-profile limits before answering printer questions or making printer-specific design and fit decisions.
---

# Printer awareness

Use observed printer facts for design constraints. A modeling request alone does not select a printer, nozzle mode, filament or build volume. Continue a visual draft when printer details are unavailable; leave print fit unverified.

## Retrieve the relevant facts

- Call \`print_studio_status\` for the configured printer, discovered devices, LAN reachability and \`slicer.profiles.root\`. A configured model is saved configuration; compare it with discovered identity before selecting a machine profile. If discovery cannot confirm it, label it configured rather than detected. Profile availability and setup \`ready\` do not establish that a particular job can print.
- Call \`print_studio_progress\` for live job state, temperatures, reported nozzle diameter, AMS trays and errors. Null, absent or incomplete fields mean unknown, not zero, empty or idle. Report when the reading was obtained; refresh live readings when answering a new status question or preparing a job. Older conversation results are historical.
- If the printer does not answer, say it is unreachable and its power state is unknown. A successful current report establishes that it is responding; failed discovery or a closed port cannot establish that it is powered off. Use \`print-studio-setup\` for missing configuration or rejected access. Do not repeatedly retry a failed probe without a relevant change.
- Read the applicable Bambu Studio machine profile under the returned \`slicer.profiles.root\` through the file/shell tools. Match the confirmed or explicitly selected model, nozzle diameter and single/dual-nozzle mode; installed profiles alone do not identify the active selection. Follow named \`inherits\` profiles and apply child and explicit job overrides. Inspect \`printable_area\`, \`printable_height\`, \`bed_exclude_area\`, nozzle-specific limits and applicable plate/process settings. Preserve the source paths and selected mode when reporting derived dimensions. If the selection or inheritance cannot be resolved, report the missing fact; ask only when it blocks the requested print decision.

Bambu Studio's documented CLI is for slicing and export, not a live-status command. Use the existing Print Studio tools for live telemetry; do not invent CLI flags or install a separate CLI to answer status questions. Keep access codes and tokens in the credential service, out of scripts, shell arguments and chat.

## Apply facts to the model

Pass \`buildVolume\` to \`viewer_load\` or \`mesh_open\` only from an applicable profile or dimensions explicitly supplied by the user. Name that source. Never substitute a familiar printer size, a test-print preset or an existing viewer box for the selected printer's limits. The viewer box is a rectangular envelope; it does not encode excluded areas or different nozzle reach. Check those separately and use the slicer for final placement validation. An inherited viewer box is not evidence of printer fit; omitting \`buildVolume\` retains an existing box, so disclose an unverified retained envelope rather than relying on it.

Do not scale or split a design to satisfy an assumed envelope. Separate the measured model dimensions from the printer constraints. A material preset describes slicing settings, not a loaded spool. Report AMS material/color/remaining values as printer-reported; unavailable remaining amounts, external-spool details and physical plate identity stay unknown. Tray IDs may repeat across AMS units in the current tool output; do not guess a physical slot or nozzle mapping from an ambiguous ID. Ask the user to identify the intended filament when that choice matters.

Checking status does not authorize heating, motion, filament loading, calibration or printing. Preserve the existing confirmation flow for sending a prepared print. Present the relevant facts and uncertainties in plain language rather than dumping raw telemetry.

## Tools and parameters

- \`print_studio_status\` takes no arguments. It returns \`configured\` (the saved printer model and serial), \`devices\` (discovered on the LAN), \`reachable\`, \`lanAccessOk\`, \`nextSteps\`, and \`slicer.profiles.root\`. Read \`nextSteps\` first and act on it; it names the one thing blocking setup.
- \`print_studio_progress\` takes no arguments. It returns live \`temperatures\` keyed by heater, \`nozzleDiameter\`, \`jobs\` with state and progress, \`ams\` trays with material, colour and remaining amount, and \`errors\`. Any field may be null when the printer did not report it.
- \`viewer_load\` and \`mesh_open\` accept \`buildVolume\` as a three-number array of millimetres. Supply it only from a resolved profile or an explicit user statement, and name the source when you report fit.
- The machine profile is a Bambu Studio JSON file under \`slicer.profiles.root\`. Read it with the file tools; \`printable_area\`, \`printable_height\`, \`bed_exclude_area\`, \`nozzle_diameter\` and \`inherits\` are the fields that decide fit.

## Worked examples

**"Is my printer on?"** Call \`print_studio_status\`, then \`print_studio_progress\`. If both answer, it is on and reachable — report the state with a timestamp. If the probe fails, say the printer is unreachable and its power state is unknown. Do not conclude it is switched off.

**"What filament is loaded?"** Call \`print_studio_progress\` and read \`ams\`. Report material and colour per tray, and say "the printer did not report a remaining amount" when that field is null. If two trays share an ID across AMS units, say the slot is ambiguous and ask which one they mean.

**"Will this fit my print bed?"** Read the machine profile for the confirmed model and nozzle, resolve \`inherits\`, and take the usable region from \`printable_area\` minus \`bed_exclude_area\`. Compare against the model's measured dimensions from \`meshState\`, not the viewer box. Report the dimension, the limit, and the margin. If the model is not selected, say fit is unverified.

**A request that must not use this skill.** "Make this bigger" is a modelling and edit request; it belongs to [blender-modeling](../blender-modeling/SKILL.md) and [mesh-edit-controls](../mesh-edit-controls/SKILL.md). Only retrieve printer facts when the answer depends on the machine — fit, feature size, clearance, material, or live state.
",
  "setup": "---
name: print-studio-setup
description: "Set up and diagnose Print Studio: Bambu Studio, H2D LAN mode, access code, and print readiness."
---

# Print Studio setup

Use this skill whenever a person asks to set up, fix, or check the 3D printer, or when
\`print_studio_status\` reports something missing. Call \`print_studio_status\` first and
work from its \`nextSteps\`; do not guess.

## What Print Studio needs

- **Bambu Studio** — the slicer for the H2D.
- **A printer on the same network** — Print Studio finds its IP address and serial
  number; the LAN access code is fetched after the adult signs in to Bambu.
- **A test slice** — a small cube, before anything is printed.

## If the slicer is missing

1. Tell the adult to press **Set up Bambu Studio** in **Plugins → Print Studio**.
2. Re-run \`print_studio_status\`; do not claim it is installed until the probe finds it.

Do not download or install applications yourself.

## Signing in does not require LAN mode

Print Studio talks to the printer's local MQTT and FTPS ports. Bambu Cloud sign-in is used only to retrieve the LAN access code; printing stays local. A printer reporting \`DevConnect: cloud\` can still accept local access after that sign-in. Only if the code is rejected should the adult enable **LAN Only Mode** on the printer, and **Developer Mode** if the firmware shows that switch.
Ask the adult to use the **Sign in with Bambu** button. Never ask them to paste account credentials into chat.

## If the printer is not configured

1. Print Studio finds the printer and its IP/serial automatically.
2. The adult signs in once with their Bambu account in **Plugins → Print Studio**;
   Print Studio fetches the printer's access code from the bound-device list. Never
   ask for the account password or the access code in chat.
3. Re-run \`print_studio_status\` and confirm the access code was accepted before
   proceeding.

If the printer is on a different network or the ports are blocked, say so and stop;
do not fall back to cloud printing.

## If the H2D profiles are missing

The H2D profiles ship inside Bambu Studio. If the probe finds none, the installed
Bambu Studio is too old for the H2D: ask the adult to update it, then re-run
\`print_studio_status\`.

## Before any print

- Use \`printer-awareness\` to resolve the selected machine profile, nozzle mode and loaded filament. Do not infer these from installed presets.
- Call the current \`meshState\` context before saying what the preview shows. Load editable USD or JavaScript models with \`viewer_load\`, or STL with \`mesh_open\`; pass only a verified build envelope. The viewer's rectangular box does not include every nozzle or exclusion constraint.
- Use \`viewer_transform\` for editable-model placement or \`mesh_transform\` for STL scene placement. Opening the same STL again adds a second copy.
- Follow \`fdm-design\` for geometry, bed attachment, supports and slicer validation. Preview checks do not establish print readiness.
- Ask the adult which loaded AMS filament to use.
- Never send a print without the adult confirming the prepared job.

## Reporting

Speak in plain language and name the one next step. Do not dump raw JSON at the person;
translate the status into "Bambu Studio is installed, the printer is not connected yet",
and so on.

## Worked examples

**First run, nothing installed.** The person says "I want to print something." Call \`print_studio_status\`, read \`nextSteps\`, and report only the first step: "Bambu Studio isn't installed yet — press Set up Bambu Studio in Plugins → Print Studio, and I'll check again." Do not list every later step.

**Installed but no printer.** "Is the printer ready?" The probe finds Bambu Studio but no configured printer. Say: "Bambu Studio is installed. I can see the printer on your network but it isn't connected yet — sign in with Bambu once and I'll take it from there." Never ask for the access code or password in chat.

**Access code rejected.** The sign-in completed but local access fails. Say the code was rejected and offer the printer-side step: enable LAN Only Mode, and Developer Mode if the firmware shows that switch. Do not retry the same probe unchanged and do not fall back to cloud printing.

**Profiles too old.** The probe finds no H2D profiles. Say the installed Bambu Studio predates the H2D and ask the adult to update it, then re-check. Do not attempt to install or patch a slicer.

**A print request, not a setup request.** "Print this." Setup is already complete, so this is a job, not a repair. Follow the Before any print checks, and use [fdm-design](../fdm-design/SKILL.md) for the geometry side. Do not re-run installation steps.

## Checking a print job before it is sent

After the setup checks pass, the order that prevents mistakes is: confirm the printer is reachable, confirm the selected machine profile matches the physical machine, confirm the filament that will actually be used, then confirm the model dimensions fit the usable region. Only then prepare the job and ask for confirmation. A job prepared against an assumed printer or an assumed spool is the failure this sequence exists to stop.
",
  "shaping": "---
name: analog-shaping
description: Bind Rumi’s built-in bulge, bend, twist, taper, and planar-cut operations after mesh-edit-controls has selected a suitable deformation.
---

# Built-in mesh operations

Use [mesh-edit-controls](../mesh-edit-controls/SKILL.md) to establish the intended change, verify it, and manage preview, save, or cancel. Use [interactive-controls](../interactive-controls/SKILL.md) for widget declarations. Built-in operations preview the existing geometry and materials locally; do not rebuild Blender while someone drags.

## Select the region

Read \`meshState\` and the original Blender source. Supply the current model’s exact \`path\`, \`revision\`, and \`url\`, a short region label, and exact mesh names in \`parts\`. An empty parts list selects every mesh in the region. Use \`viewer_highlight\` to establish a deformation region; cuts use the automatic cut-plane guide instead.

\`center\` is model-local XYZ millimetres. The local frame has N = \`direction\`, U = world-up cross N, and V = N cross U; near a vertical N, world-up is Y instead of Z. \`radii\` are extents along U, V, N. Choose a region that excludes protected features. Influence is compact and zero outside this ellipsoid. The ellipsoid limits deformations, not cuts.

These operations do not implement extrusion, curved cuts, remeshing, or beveling. For other variations that preserve connectivity, follow the field workflow in \`mesh-edit-controls\`; do not relabel an unrelated operation.

## Deformation values

Supply all six \`values\` in 0..1, but expose only axes useful for this edit. Declare axis ranges as min 0 and max 1.

- Bulge uses \`amount\`, \`angle\`, \`spread\`, \`peakX\`, \`peakY\`, and \`falloff\`.
- Bend, twist, and taper use all except \`angle\`. Their \`limit\` does not set an angle range.
- Cut uses \`amount\`, \`angle\`, and \`spread\`; supply .5 for the unused values.

For deformations, amount .5 leaves the geometry unchanged; larger values deform positively and smaller values negatively. For bulge, \`limit\` is maximum displacement in millimetres, no greater than the smallest radius. Spread .5 gives the supplied extents; peakX and peakY .5 center the effect. Angle .5 points outward. Falloff ranges from broad to concentrated; .35 is a useful starting value.

Start with zero deformation unless the person requested an initial change. Define reset values explicitly: amount, spread, angle, and peak normally reset to .5; choose falloff’s reset to match the intended starting profile. Reset affects only that control’s axes.

## Planar cuts

For a bottom cut, use \`operation: cut\`, \`direction: [0,0,1]\` to retain the upper side, and \`center\` at the proposed height. Amount moves the plane by \`(amount*2-1)*limit\` along direction; .5 is the proposed height. Angle tilts along V and spread leans along U, each spanning -60 to +60 degrees with .5 untilted. Positive tilt raises the plane toward positive V; positive lean raises it toward positive U. Set every radius at least as large as limit; radii do not clip the cut footprint. Select all parts that should share the edge.

Opening a cut clears prior Gaussian highlights and displays a continuous teal guide at the actual plane. A rendered cut receipt confirms the guide and controls. The guide disappears on freeze or close; other region highlights are rejected until the cut resolves. The viewer previews capped cross-sections; saving uses a Blender intersection that preserves cavity openings.

Open controls before applying the requested cut. To restore material below an already cut edge, use the matching earlier revision.

## Change an open edit

\`meshState\` contains the last released values. Cursor snapshots and measurements during an edit do not describe a trustworthy deformed selection. Close the edit before changing its region; open a fresh session for a new direction, operation, or region. Follow \`mesh-edit-controls\` to preserve wanted adjustments or discard a rejected preview. Close restores the saved original and clears the region highlight.

## Worked examples

**Bulge an eye.** "Make the eyes bulge out a bit." Read \`meshState\`, then open \`operation: bulge\` with a \`center\` at the eye region in model-local millimetres, \`direction\` as the outward normal, and \`radii\` covering the eye but stopping short of the socket rim. Expose \`amount\` only, max 1, starting at .5, with \`limit\` set to the smallest radius and no more. Check the rim has not moved and both eyes match.

**Bend a flat panel.** "Curve this sign panel." Use \`operation: bend\` with \`direction\` along the bend axis, a region spanning the panel, and \`values\` supplied for all six fields with unused ones at .5. Expose \`amount\` and expose \`falloff\` only if the person wants to tune how local the bend is. Verify the panel's mounting edge stayed planar.

**Taper a post.** "Make the post thinner at the top." \`operation: taper\` with a vertical region and radii reaching past the post's section. Amount below .5 narrows positively along the shaping direction; confirm which way the geometry moved before presenting. Check the base where it meets the plate has not thinned.

**A planar cut.** "Cut the bottom off flat." Use \`operation: cut\`, \`direction: [0,0,1]\` to keep the upper side, \`center\` at the proposed height, and radii at least as large as \`limit\` for every part that should share the edge. Amount .5 is the proposed height. Confirm the guide traces one continuous teal plane before the person commits.

**A request that needs a field instead.** "Squeeze the waist in but keep the ribs sharp." A bulge would round the ribs, and spread alone cannot keep an edge. Point to the displacement-field workflow in [mesh-edit-controls](../mesh-edit-controls/SKILL.md) rather than relabelling a bulge.

**Wrong region after opening.** The person says "not that part, the other one". Close first; the region cannot be changed on an open edit. Then reopen with a new region and the same operation and starting values.
",
  "stateToolAvailable": false,
